<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Deliberately NOT sandboxed (PLAN.md §7.1). The product is "let Claude Code touch the
	     file on disk", and a sandboxed app cannot do that without fighting security-scoped
	     bookmarks at every turn. We are not shipping to the Mac App Store; we ship Developer
	     ID signed, notarised, with the hardened runtime on.

	     The real boundary is not the sandbox — it is workspace grants (PLAN.md §7.2), enforced
	     in SheetStore and applied to every path the MCP server touches. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<false/>

	<!-- Kept even while unsandboxed: we already persist security-scoped bookmarks from
	     NSOpenPanel, so a future sandboxed build is a build-setting change rather than a
	     rewrite. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.files.bookmarks.app-scope</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- The tripwire this comment used to be said: "Nothing here grants network access, JIT,
	     unsigned memory, or library-validation bypass. If a future change appears to need one
	     of those, that is a design smell worth arguing about before it is worth entitling."

	     The argument happened. Cloud Share (2026-08) is the one exception, and it is entitled
	     below rather than quietly: with the owner's switch on, the app holds an outbound
	     WebSocket to a relay and pumps a local `opensheets-mcp` subprocess's stdio through it,
	     so a browser-based assistant can reach the folders the owner granted. The counter-
	     argument is in DOCUMENTATION.md §5.9, which used to say a hosted bridge was the wrong
	     shape, and the answer is that the relay routes bytes and grants nothing: every grant
	     check, the deny-list, and the untrusted-content envelope still run in the same local
	     process they always ran in, and the relay holds token *hashes* only.

	     What this key does not buy: it is inert while `app-sandbox` is false, because an
	     unsandboxed process already has the network. It is here for the same reason the two
	     file keys above are — so a future sandboxed build stays a build-setting change rather
	     than a rewrite — and so this file states what the app does instead of understating it.

	     The other three still stand. Nothing here grants JIT, unsigned memory, or
	     library-validation bypass, and a change that appears to need one of those is still a
	     design smell worth arguing about before it is worth entitling. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
